  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Eight-toothed Cuckoo Leaf-cutter Bee (Coelioxys octodentatus) is a small, dark-colored parasitic bee that belongs to the Megachilidae family, which also includes mason, leafcutter, carder, and resin bees. Unlike its relatives, this species is a cuckoo bee that lays eggs in the nests of other bees rather than constructing its own nests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Eight Teeth on Abdomen",
      "description": "The distinctive feature giving this species its name is the presence of eight sharp, tooth-like projections along the tip of its abdomen, visible when viewed from above.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Dark Body",
      "description": "This bee is relatively small with a predominantly dark brown to black body, smaller than most leafcutter bee species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Smooth Abdomen Texture",
      "description": "The abdomen appears smooth and shiny with sparse hair compared to the fuzzy appearance of pollen-collecting bees.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Eight-toothed Cuckoo Leaf-cutter Bees are found throughout New England in areas where their host bees nest, including gardens, woodlands, and meadows with dead stems or existing cavities.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a cuckoo bee, Coelioxys octodentatus practices brood parasitism by sneaking into the nests of Megachile leafcutter bees and laying its eggs, allowing the host bee to provide all the care for the cuckoo bee's offspring.",
